# Local development only. These credentials never leave your machine — the
# container is not exposed beyond localhost and nothing here is a real secret.
# Copy to .env to override; every default below is also baked into
# docker-compose.yml and scripts/lib.sh, so .env is optional.

# Container superuser. Used only by initdb and by the test fixture loader
# (fixtures are seeded out-of-band on purpose — see db/tests/000_fixtures.sql).
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres

# DDL / migration owner. Owns every table; migrations connect as this role.
FS_OWNER_PASSWORD=owner_dev_password

# The application role. This is the only role the API ever connects as.
FS_APP_PASSWORD=app_dev_password

# The control-plane role (CLAUDE.md §7.1). Separate application, separate
# origin, separate credentials — never the app role with extra grants.
FS_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin_dev_password

# The mail sender (M8). Reads and drains the outbox and can touch nothing
# else — the bodies hold live reset links, which is why app_role cannot read
# them and why this is a role of its own.
FS_MAIL_PASSWORD=mail_dev_password

FS_DB_NAME=flightsquare
FS_DB_PORT=5432

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Platform billing (§8.3) — what a tenant pays FlightSquare. Web only.
#
# Leave both unset and the API runs the stub provider: it signs and delivers
# the same events to the same webhook, so checkout, a plan change, a failed
# payment and a cancellation at period end are all exercisable here with no
# account and no card. Setting a key is the whole of the switch to Stripe.
#
#   FS_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
#   FS_STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...        # printed by `stripe listen`
#
# With real keys, forward events to the local API:
#   stripe listen --forward-to 127.0.0.1:3000/webhooks/stripe
#
# Stripe needs a recurring Price per sellable plan, each with the lookup key
# in plans.price_lookup_key — `pro_monthly` and `enterprise_monthly` today. A
# lookup key rather than a price id because the id differs between test and
# live and a migration cannot know which one it is running against.

# Where this API answers from the outside; the stub builds its checkout and
# portal URLs against it, and a browser visits them. 127.0.0.1, not
# localhost — web/next.config.ts only trusts the former in development.
# FS_API_PUBLIC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The mail sender (M8): `npm run mail -w api`
#
# With no API key the worker logs each message and marks it delivered, which
# is a real pass through the same code a real transport takes. Development
# reads the links with ./scripts/outbox.sh.
#
#   FS_MAIL_API_KEY=re_...                    # Resend
#   FS_MAIL_FROM="FlightSquare <no-reply@yourdomain>"
#
# Tuning, all optional:
#   FS_MAIL_POLL_SECONDS=10     how often to look when the last look found none
#   FS_MAIL_BATCH=20            how many to take in one pass
#   FS_MAIL_MAX_ATTEMPTS=8      after this, a message waits for a person
